    title: "Trump administration makes another appeal to the Supreme Court to allow mail ballot restrictions",
    excerpt:
      "Envelopes for mail-in ballots at the Wake County Board of Elections are seen on Thursday, Sept. 3, 2026, in Raleigh, N.C.",
    category: "politics",
    author: "riley-santos",
    date: "2026-07-27",
    readTime: 5,
    image: "pop-09",
    featured: false,
    content: [
      "WASHINGTON  — The Trump administration on Sunday revived its plea to the Supreme Court after a federal court barred additional mail-ballot restrictions before the midterms.",
      "The filing comes after U.S. District Court Judge Indira Talwani issued an order extending a restraining order against the Postal Service to implement President Donald Trump’s executive order for the November elections.",
      "The legal fight is occurring as states prepare to send out mail ballots, leaving little time for the administration to make major changes in mail vote laws.",
      "“Ballots are already being mailed in North Carolina and more States will begin the mailing process as the injunction remains in place — including Alabama on September 9 and at least five States the week of September 13,” Solicitor General John Sauer stated in the petition.",
      "“Once those ballot envelopes get into the mailstream there’s no way to get them back. And while the injunction permits the Postal Service and the States to continue to take steps to ensure that their envelopes meet the Rule’s requirements ... each day the injunction remains in place, the risk of sowing confusion and chaos grows because the injunction renders those preparatory steps voluntary, rather than mandatory.”",
      "This is the administration’s third request for the top court to step in. The high court has permitted the proposal to go ahead but has not ruled on its legality.",
      "The fight might have enormous implications for this year’s elections, when control of Congress is at stake. Almost one-third of voters in the U.S. vote by mail.",
      "Democratic states and voting rights groups argue the reforms violate the constitution. Election officials say they don't have enough time to change their systems to comply with the new Postal Service orders.",
      "The Trump administration has characterized the reforms as common-sense regulations that it has the authority to regulate the mail.",
      "“Put simply, the best way to address the fear that respondents have that they won’t be able to comply with the Rule is to grant an immediate administrative stay of the order, and then a full stay pending further review,” Sauer said. “This will sweep away the improper cloud of uncertainty the district court has cast over the Rule, making it clear to all concerned that compliance with the Rule is not optional and must begin without further delay.”",
      "“State law will continue to govern eligibility for mail-in and absentee voting and state and local officials will determine who is eligible to use the method and which ballots are eligible to be counted,” the brief also said. “The Postal Service will not be involved in determining voter eligibility, maintaining voter rolls or counting ballots,” the agency said.",
      "Trump has long tried to curtail the use of mail voting, even as he routinely votes by mail himself. He wrongly blamed mail-in balloting for his loss in the 2020 election to Democrat Joe Biden, promoting unsubstantiated charges of fraud. But he has not yet been able to overturn the long-standing vote mechanism.",
     
    ],
